\section*{Extended States with Extensive Disorder}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a mathematical result relevant to this question in the selected manuscript. For uniform iid Anderson site disorder in every fixed dimension at least three, sufficiently small fixed disorder is claimed to give purely absolutely continuous spectrum on a nonempty open interval.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A finite-volume matrix-state certificate is propagated through controlled ordinary sweeps. Local trials and clearing of pending components maintain the comparison forms needed for resolvent bounds, which identify an interval with nonzero absolutely continuous weight.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The theorem supplies a purely absolutely continuous window for iid uniform disorder in every d>=3. The problem statement bundles a broader extended-state/continuous-spectrum existence discussion with the borderline d=2 question, and the latter is not answered by this manuscript. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 261, the selected planar Anderson statement constructs the self-adjoint operator and gives its almost-sure spectral interval. Spectral type, including the claimed pure-point conclusion, is not contained in it. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

\subsection{Sources}
